  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "none; the full paper text is present.",
    "relation": "uses_without_test",
    "tranche": "T4",
    "tranche_reason": "The paper measures routing similarity across refusal/compliance and other query comparisons, but it does not test the difference-in-means refusal direction or any of the listed claim versions. Its steering experiments deliberately induce harmfulness and therefore do not establish how refusal behaves without that intervention.",
    "gate_note": "The paper reports routing-similarity measurements for refusal/compliance and query comparisons; these do not measure whether a refusal direction mediates refusal. Its harmfulness-steering ASR and MMLU results use deliberately applied perturbations, not the claimed refusal-direction ablation or addition, so they are not rows for the claim. Hyperparameter-selection results are also not included.",
    "test_reason": "The paper uses contrastive harmful-vs-benign steering, including a MeanDiff estimator, as a tool to induce harmful compliance in MoE models. It reports only its own pipeline's ASR against baselines. It never ablates or adds the Arditi refusal direction as a test of mediation. The routing probes measure expert-routing similarity, not refusal or a refusal direction. Record's T6/discusses understates the tool use but correctly finds no version tested.",
    "authors_conclusion": "Correct. The authors conclude that perturbation-induced routing drift weakens conventional steering in MoE models. They find routing tracks query semantics more than refusal behaviour or safety intent, and propose RARE (router-nullspace projection plus downstream correction) as giving a better efficacy–utility trade-off.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "RARE uses multiple contrastive perturbation estimators, router-nullspace projection, and downstream routing correction; it does not ablate or add the original refusal direction.",
    "method_sensitivity": "Routing similarity differs across comparison conditions: same-query refusal/compliance and within-task response-mode comparisons show lower divergence than cross-query or cross-task comparisons. Matched harmful–benign pairs also show lower divergence than random-pair controls.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "Same-query teacher-forced refusal vs compliance continuations show higher Top-8 expert overlap and lower router JSD than cross-query refusal controls in GPT-oss, Qwen3-30B and DeepSeek-V2.",
        "proposed_claim": "In MoE models, refusal vs compliance behaviour changes little in expert routing compared with query-content changes.",
        "row_ids": [
          "r1",
          "r2",
          "r3",
          "r4",
          "r5",
          "r6",
          "r7",
          "r8",
